.Dd September 16, 2026
.Dt NDI_HOLD_DEVI 9F
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ndi_hold_devi ,
.Nm ndi_rele_devi
.Nd hold and release device-node references
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/sunndi.h
.Ft "void"
.Fo ndi_hold_devi
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fc
.Ft "void"
.Fo ndi_rele_devi
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fc
.Sh INTERFACE LEVEL
illumos specific
.Pq Consolidation Private .
.Sh PARAMETERS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va dip
Valid device node to hold or release.
.El
.Sh DESCRIPTION
\fBndi_hold_devi()\fP increments the device-node reference count of \fBdip\fP.
While a reference is held, normal device-tree operations cannot detach and
destroy the referenced node.
The caller must already ensure that the node cannot disappear while the hold
is being acquired, for example by owning the parent's critical section.
Taking a reference to an unprotected pointer obtained earlier is not safe.
.Pp
\fBndi_rele_devi()\fP releases one reference.
Every hold must have a matching release, including references explicitly
returned by configuration functions.
The caller must not use the pointer after its final reference is released
unless some other protection keeps the node alive.
.Pp
These functions neither serialize access to the children of \fBdip\fP nor load
its driver module.
Use \fBndi_devi_enter()\fP for child configuration and \fBndi_hold_driver()\fP for a
driver-module reference.
.Sh CONTEXT
These functions may be called from user, kernel, or low-level interrupt
context.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions have no return value.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ndi_devi_enter 9F ,
.Xr ndi_hold_driver 9F ,
.Xr sunndi 9F
